{"name":"Turmeric Root","hex":"#feae0d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"turmeric","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Indian plant (Curcuma longa), with aromatic rhizomes, part of the ginger family (Zingiberaceae).","The pulverized rhizome of the turmeric plant, used for flavoring and to add a bright yellow color to food."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turmeric"},{"term":"root","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of a plant, generally underground, that anchors and supports the plant body, absorbs and stores water and nutrients, and in some plants is able to perform vegetative reproduction.","A root vegetable."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow roots; to enter the earth, as roots; to take root and begin to grow.","To prepare, oversee, or otherwise cause the rooting of cuttings."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/root"}],"entries":[{"title":"Turmeric","blocks":[{"text":"Turmeric, or Curcuma longa, is a flowering plant in the ginger family Zingiberaceae. It is a perennial, rhizomatous, herbaceous plant native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia that requires temperatures between 20 and 30 °C and high annual rainfall to thrive. Plants are gathered each year for their rhizomes, some for propagation in the following season and some for consumption or dyeing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Turmeric_inflorescence.jpg/330px-Turmeric_inflorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turmeric"},{"title":"Root","blocks":[{"text":"In vascular plants, the roots are the organs of a plant that are modified to provide anchorage for the plant and take in water and nutrients into the plant body, which allows plants to grow taller and faster. They are most often below the surface of the soil, but roots can also be aerial or aerating, that is, growing up above the ground or especially above water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg/330px-Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg/500px-Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg/1280px-Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg/500px-Curcuma_longa_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACurcuma_longa_roots.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Curcuma_longa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-199.jpg/330px-Curcuma_longa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-199.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Curcuma_longa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-199.jpg/330px-Curcuma_longa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-199.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Botanical view of Curcuma longa","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACurcuma_longa_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-199.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_L.0939330_-_Bernecker%2C_A._-_Curcuma_domestica_Valeton_-_Artwork.jpeg/250px-Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_L.0939330_-_Bernecker%2C_A._-_Curcuma_domestica_Valeton_-_Artwork.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t